  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another round of minor coastal flooding is expected \nalong the coast and lower Cape Fear River during the daytime high \ntide today, with the potential for another round on Wednesday, \nespecially along the lower Cape Fear River.\n\nAlthough positive tidal departures are diminishing, high \nastronomical tides will continue to lead to more minor coastal \nflooding along the coast and lower Cape Fear River during today's \ndaytime high tide. There is low confidence for additional tidal \nflooding along the coast after today, although confidence is \nmoderate to high for more tidal flooding along the lower Cape Fear \nRiver.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warming trend kicks off Tuesday, allowing\ntemperatures to soar above seasonal norms. Next earnest rain\nchances return by this weekend.\n\n500 mb heights start picking up Tuesday, with ridging starting\nto poke up from the Gulf. This kicks off the start of above\nnormal temperatures that appear to last Tuesday through\nSaturday. Highs in the mid-to-upper 80s looks like a slam dunk\nfor most. Highs around 90F appear possible along and west of\nI-95 as early as Wednesday, with those chances solidifying more\nby Thursday and Friday (could potentially be 90-92F in some\nspots). Moisture parameters pick up as well, with dewpoints\nsoaring into the 60s, and even into the lower 70s for the\ncoastal areas Thursday through Saturday. Friday in particular\ncould be the most hot and humid day of the week, with the heat\nindex climbing up towards 95 in some spots (in October, mind\nyou).\n\nPattern starts to flip by Saturday. The heat ridge starts to\npush offshore, while a very broad trough pattern that stretches\nfrom near the PNW all the way to New England helps to suppress\nthe ridge southward. Weak shortwave energy breaks off the broad\ntrough and will help produce scattered showers and perhaps a few\nrumbles of thunder Saturday, but nothing appears to be a washout\nat this time. These rain chances appear to get a bit more\nbullish by Sunday and Monday, as part of the broad trough in the\nupper Midwest amplifies and sweeps through the Southeast as a\nmore organized frontal system. Still much to debate about the\ntiming and organization of this system, along with what kind of\nrainfall amounts to expect.",
